'use strict';
const TOPICS = [
 {name:'Earth’s spheres',lp:'LP1',intro:'Describe each sphere and explain how a change in one can affect another.',notes:[['Four parts of one system','The <b>atmosphere</b> is the gases around Earth. The <b>geosphere</b> is the rocks, minerals and solid Earth. The <b>hydrosphere</b> is all Earth’s water. The <b>biosphere</b> is living things and the places where life exists.'],['The spheres overlap','A tree is part of the biosphere, its water belongs to the hydrosphere, and the air around it belongs to the atmosphere. Soil connects mineral material, water, air and organisms.'],['Interactions have a direction','Name the cause, then the effect. For example: roots grow into cracks and break rock. The biosphere affects the geosphere. A useful explanation says <b>what happens</b>, as well as naming the spheres.'],['The atmosphere supports life','It supplies gases used by living things, helps keep Earth warm and provides protection from harmful ultraviolet radiation.']],fix:'Water vapour is water in the gas state. It belongs to the hydrosphere even while it is mixed with gases in the atmosphere.',recall:[['A wētā shelters between stones. Which two spheres are represented by the animal and the stones?','The wētā belongs to the biosphere; the stones belong to the geosphere.'],['How could a plant change the atmosphere?','Photosynthesis removes carbon dioxide from the air. Plants can also release water vapour through transpiration.']]},
 {name:'Water on the move',lp:'LP1–2',intro:'Trace pathways, name changes of state and identify what drives water movement.',notes:[['Water can change state','<b>Evaporation:</b> liquid to gas, with energy absorbed. <b>Condensation:</b> gas to liquid, with energy released. <b>Melting:</b> solid to liquid, with energy absorbed. <b>Freezing:</b> liquid to solid, with energy released.'],['Movement without a state change','<b>Runoff</b> flows across the surface. <b>Infiltration</b> enters the soil from the surface; water can then percolate deeper. <b>Groundwater flow</b> moves through spaces in rock or sediment. These movements do not require a change of state.'],['Water travels through plants','Roots take up liquid water. During <b>transpiration</b>, water evaporates within leaves and water vapour leaves through small openings. Solar energy supports this transfer.'],['Sunlight and gravity','Solar energy supplies energy for evaporation and can contribute to melting. Gravity pulls precipitation down and helps drive runoff, rivers and groundwater movement. <b>Precipitation</b> is water falling from clouds, as rain, snow, sleet or hail.']],fix:'A water molecule has many possible routes. There is no single starting point and no compulsory order that every molecule must follow.',recall:[['Rain lands on a path and runs into a drain. Has the water changed state?','No. It remains liquid as it moves across the surface by runoff.'],['An ice cube becomes water and the water later becomes vapour. What are the processes and energy changes?','Melting, then evaporation. Both require water to absorb energy.']]},
 {name:'Solar heating',lp:'LP3',intro:'Connect the angle of sunlight to surface heating, particle energy and evaporation.',notes:[['Latitude and sunlight','<b>Latitude</b> is angular distance north or south of the equator. The equator is 0°. Sunlight generally reaches lower latitudes more directly and higher latitudes at a lower angle. The actual angle also varies with time of day and season.'],['Same energy across a different area','A more direct beam covers a smaller area, so more energy reaches each square metre. A lower-angle beam spreads the same energy over a larger area, giving less heating per square metre.'],['Particles and evaporation','As water warms, its particles gain average kinetic energy and move faster. More particles at the surface can escape the liquid as invisible water vapour. Warmer water usually evaporates faster when other conditions are the same.'],['The torch investigation','Change the angle to the paper and measure the illuminated area. Keep brightness, torch-to-patch distance and room lighting the same. Repeat readings to check variation; a mean reduces the influence of random variation. Repeats cannot automatically fix a faulty method.']],fix:'The equator’s warmth is mainly explained by the angle and concentration of incoming sunlight, rather than a meaningful difference in distance from the Sun. Evaporation can happen below boiling point.',recall:[['Two equal beams cover 10 and 30 squares. Which gives more heating to each square?','The beam covering 10 squares. Its energy is concentrated into a smaller area.'],['Why can washing still dry on a cool day?','Some surface particles still have enough energy to escape as vapour. Evaporation can occur at low temperatures, although other conditions affect its rate.']]},
 {name:'Clouds and rain',lp:'LP4',intro:'Explain the link between lower pressure, expansion, cooling and condensation.',notes:[['Rising air expands','Atmospheric pressure decreases with height. A rising parcel of moist air expands as the pressure around it drops. This expansion cools the air.'],['Cooling allows condensation','If the air cools enough, water vapour changes from gas into tiny liquid droplets. This is <b>condensation</b>. Tiny particles such as dust or sea salt provide surfaces for droplets to form on.'],['Cloud droplets and rain','Lots of tiny suspended droplets make a cloud visible. Clouds can also contain ice crystals. Droplets can collide and join; when drops become large enough, they fall as rain under gravity.'],['Humidity and the bottle model','Humidity describes water vapour in the air. In the classroom bottle, releasing pressure lets the air expand and cool. Vapour condenses onto tiny particles, producing a visible cloud. The bottle models some processes but not the size or complexity of the atmosphere.']],fix:'Water vapour is invisible. The visible cloud consists of liquid droplets or ice crystals. Forming a liquid cloud involves condensation; freezing changes liquid water into solid ice.',recall:[['What connects an expanding parcel of air to the appearance of droplets?','Expansion cools the air. If it cools enough, water vapour condenses to form droplets.'],['Why were tiny particles useful in the bottle demonstration?','They provided surfaces for water vapour to condense onto, helping droplets form.']]},
 {name:'Mountains and rainfall',lp:'LP4',intro:'Use wind direction and air movement to explain a wet side and a rain shadow.',notes:[['The windward side','The side facing the incoming wind is <b>windward</b>. Mountains force moist air to rise. The rising air expands and cools, allowing condensation and cloud formation. Drops grow and precipitation may fall.'],['The leeward side','The air passes over the mountains with some moisture already lost as rain. On the <b>leeward</b> side it descends and warms. This makes cloud formation less likely and can evaporate droplets, giving a drier <b>rain shadow</b>.'],['A New Zealand example','With moist westerly winds from the Tasman Sea, the western Southern Alps receive high rainfall and Canterbury lies on the drier eastern side. The wind direction matters when identifying the windward side.'],['Build a linked explanation','Use a cause at each step: mountains force air upwards; lower pressure allows expansion; expansion causes cooling; enough cooling leads to condensation; droplets grow and fall. Then explain descending, warming air on the other side.']],fix:'Mountains force air upwards. They do not squeeze water out of clouds. A rain shadow is relatively dry; it can still receive rain.',recall:[['If moist air arrives from the east, which slope faces the wind?','The eastern slope. Windward means facing the incoming wind, so it changes when wind direction changes.'],['Why does descending air usually produce less cloud?','Descending air warms. It is less likely to reach the conditions needed for condensation, and remaining droplets may evaporate.']]},
 {name:'Water stores and time',lp:'LP5',intro:'Distinguish a store from a pathway and explain why recycled water can still be scarce.',notes:[['A finite supply','Earth has approximately the same total amount of water over human timescales. The water cycle recycles existing water. About <b>97% of all Earth’s water</b> is saltwater and about <b>3%</b> is freshwater.'],['Freshwater is unevenly stored','Of <b>freshwater only</b>, about 68.7% is in glaciers and ice sheets and about 30.1% is fresh groundwater. These have a different denominator from the 97% figure. A large store may be frozen, deep underground or slow to replenish.'],['Residence time is an average','A <b>store</b> is a place that holds water. A <b>pathway</b> transfers it. <b>Residence time</b> is the average time water remains in a store. Individual molecules can leave sooner or later. Times vary with the location and conditions.'],['Local water can run short','A region’s accessible water supply decreases if losses exceed inputs. Evaporated water may travel away and rainfall may return much later or elsewhere. Deep groundwater can recharge slowly. A simplified model needs inputs, outputs and timescales to predict change.']],fix:'Water in an underground store still belongs to the hydrosphere. The rock holding it belongs to the geosphere. Recycling water does not guarantee enough fresh water in the place and at the time people need it.',recall:[['An average residence time is 10 days. Must a particular molecule leave after 10 days?','No. Ten days is an average across the store, not a countdown for each molecule.'],['A town uses 90 units each week but only 60 are replaced. What happens to the store?','It falls by 30 units each week under these simplified conditions. Existing water is moved or used; it is not destroyed.']]}
];

// All retrieval prompts are written for this revision resource.
const BANK = [
 [0,'A tree root widens a crack in a paving stone. Which interaction describes this?', ['Biosphere affecting geosphere','Atmosphere affecting biosphere','Geosphere affecting atmosphere','Hydrosphere affecting atmosphere'],0,'The living root belongs to the biosphere and changes the solid stone in the geosphere.'],
 [0,'Which pair consists entirely of things in the geosphere?', ['Granite and beach sand','Rain and granite','Fern and beach sand','Air and basalt'],0,'Rocks, minerals and sand are parts of the geosphere.'],
 [0,'A snail needs oxygen from the air. Which sphere supplies that oxygen?', ['Hydrosphere','Geosphere','Atmosphere','Biosphere'],2,'The atmosphere is the mixture of gases surrounding Earth, including oxygen.'],
 [0,'Where should underground liquid water be included when describing Earth’s spheres?', ['Only the atmosphere','The hydrosphere','Only the biosphere','It is outside all four spheres'],1,'The hydrosphere includes all water, including groundwater. The surrounding rock is part of the geosphere.'],
 [1,'A wet footprint dries without the ground reaching 100°C. What has happened?', ['The water has evaporated','The water has frozen','The water has been destroyed','The water has become oxygen'],0,'Surface particles can escape as vapour below boiling point. Evaporation does not require boiling.'],
 [1,'Rainwater enters a lawn through its surface. Which process is this?', ['Precipitation','Condensation','Infiltration','Melting'],2,'Infiltration is the entry of water from the surface into soil.'],
 [1,'Which transfer can happen while the water stays liquid throughout?', ['Frost melting','Water flowing along a stream','Vapour forming droplets','Water evaporating from a leaf'],1,'Stream flow moves liquid water without requiring a change of state. Gravity helps drive it downhill.'],
 [1,'Water in a freezer tray turns into ice. Which statement explains the change?', ['Energy is absorbed during melting','Energy is released during freezing','Energy is absorbed during evaporation','Gravity turns the water into ice'],1,'Freezing changes liquid into solid as energy is transferred away from the water.'],
 [2,'The same light beam is spread across a bigger patch. How does energy per square centimetre change?', ['It increases','It stays the same','It decreases','It becomes zero'],2,'The same total energy is shared across more area, so each square centimetre receives less.'],
 [2,'Which torch arrangement models sunlight striking a surface most directly?', ['A beam at 90° to the surface','A beam nearly parallel to the surface','A torch pointing away from the surface','A torch with its light switched off'],0,'At 90° to the surface the beam is perpendicular, producing a smaller, more concentrated patch.'],
 [2,'When liquid water warms, what happens to the particles on average?', ['They stop moving','They lose all their energy','They gain kinetic energy','Each particle becomes physically larger'],2,'Higher temperature means greater average kinetic energy, so particles move faster on average.'],
 [2,'A student records three patch areas at one torch angle: 20, 22 and 21 squares. What is the mean?', ['20 squares','21 squares','22 squares','63 squares'],1,'Add the areas and divide by three: (20 + 22 + 21) ÷ 3 = 21 squares.'],
 [3,'After the bottle pressure is released, what directly causes the air to cool?', ['The expanding air','Smoke changing into water','The bottle becoming a mountain','The water disappearing'],0,'The pressure drop lets the air expand, and expansion cools it.'],
 [3,'What can water vapour condense onto when cloud droplets form?', ['Only large leaves','Small dust or sea salt particles','Only the ground','Sunlight'],1,'Tiny particles in the air provide surfaces on which droplets can form.'],
 [3,'A visible white mist forms when moist air cools. What makes the mist visible?', ['Individual invisible gas molecules','Tiny suspended liquid droplets','New oxygen formed from water','Water molecules becoming larger'],1,'Tiny droplets scatter light. Water vapour itself is invisible.'],
 [3,'Which event turns small cloud droplets into falling rain?', ['Droplets become oxygen','Droplets stop moving permanently','Droplets grow large enough to fall','All the water in the cloud boils'],2,'Droplets can join and grow. Large enough drops fall under gravity.'],
 [4,'Moist air blows from the ocean towards a coastal mountain. What happens first at the mountain slope?', ['The air is forced upwards','The air freezes instantly','The air stops containing water','The mountain squeezes the cloud'],0,'The mountain acts as a barrier, forcing the incoming air to rise.'],
 [4,'An easterly wind reaches a mountain range. Which side is windward?', ['The western side','The eastern side','Both sides are always leeward','The summit only'],1,'An easterly wind comes from the east, so the eastern side faces the incoming wind.'],
 [4,'Which description is most accurate for air descending into a rain shadow?', ['It expands and cools further','It warms as it descends','It always produces heavy rain','It has lost every water molecule'],1,'Descending air is compressed and warms. It has often already lost some moisture as precipitation.'],
 [4,'A forecast describes a rain shadow. What should you expect?', ['It can never rain there','More rain than every other location','Relatively low rainfall under that wind pattern','The water cycle has stopped'],2,'Rain shadows are relatively dry under the relevant conditions, but they can still receive rainfall.'],
 [5,'Which is a water pathway rather than a water store?', ['An ice sheet','A lake','Transpiration','An underground aquifer'],2,'Transpiration transfers water into the atmosphere. The others are places where water is held.'],
 [5,'Why can a very large underground water store still be vulnerable to overuse?', ['It has no water molecules','Replacement may be slower than removal','Large stores create water automatically','Every aquifer refills overnight'],1,'Store size and recharge rate are different. A store shrinks if removal exceeds replacement.'],
 [5,'What does “about 3% of Earth’s water is freshwater” compare?', ['Freshwater with all Earth’s water','Glacier water with freshwater only','River water with groundwater only','Saltwater with ice only'],0,'The denominator is all Earth’s water. Most of the remaining approximately 97% is saltwater.'],
 [5,'A simplified tank starts with 500 L, gains 80 L and loses 120 L. What is left?', ['700 L','540 L','460 L','300 L'],2,'Final store = starting water + input − output = 500 + 80 − 120 = 460 L.']
].map((q,id)=>({id,topic:q[0],prompt:q[1],options:q[2],correct:q[3],feedback:q[4]}));

const EXPLAIN = [
 {topic:0,prompt:'A bird drinks from a shallow pool and then leaves footprints in soft mud. Describe two interactions between Earth’s spheres.',help:'Name the spheres and the effect in each interaction.',answer:'The hydrosphere supplies water to a living bird in the biosphere. The bird, part of the biosphere, then changes the soft ground in the geosphere by making footprints.',criteria:['Pool water and bird linked to hydrosphere and biosphere','Bird and ground linked to biosphere and geosphere','An effect described for each interaction']},
 {topic:1,prompt:'Explain two different routes by which rainwater in a school garden could return to the atmosphere.',help:'Include a direct route and a route through a plant.',answer:'Water at the surface can absorb energy and evaporate directly into the air as vapour. Alternatively, water can infiltrate the soil, be absorbed by roots and move through a plant. It evaporates within the leaves and enters the atmosphere during transpiration.',criteria:['Direct evaporation explained','Infiltration and root uptake included in the plant route','Transpiration described as release of water vapour']},
 {topic:2,prompt:'Two identical trays of water receive equal light energy. The beam covers a small area on one tray and a larger area on the other. Explain which is likely to evaporate faster if other conditions are equal.',help:'Link area, energy concentration, particle motion and escape.',answer:'The smaller illuminated area receives more energy per unit area, so the water there warms more. Its particles gain average kinetic energy and move faster. More surface particles can escape as water vapour, so evaporation is likely to be faster there under these conditions.',criteria:['Smaller area linked to greater energy per unit area','Greater heating linked to particle kinetic energy','Faster evaporation linked to more particles escaping the surface']},
 {topic:3,prompt:'A parcel of moist air rises until a cloud begins to form. Explain the changes that lead to the cloud.',help:'Begin with the change in surrounding pressure.',answer:'Pressure decreases as the air rises. The air expands and cools. If it cools enough, invisible water vapour condenses onto tiny particles, forming liquid droplets. Many tiny droplets make the cloud visible.',criteria:['Lower surrounding pressure','Expansion and cooling linked','Condensation described as gas to liquid','Visible cloud linked to tiny droplets']},
 {topic:4,prompt:'During an easterly wind, a weather station on the eastern slope records more rain than one on the western slope. Explain this pattern.',help:'Use windward, cooling, condensation and descending air.',answer:'The eastern side faces the incoming wind, so it is windward. Moist air is forced up the eastern slope, expands and cools. Water vapour condenses into droplets that grow and fall as rain. On the western side the air descends and warms after losing some moisture, creating a drier rain shadow.',criteria:['Eastern side identified as windward','Rising air linked to expansion and cooling','Condensation and rainfall linked','Western descending air linked to warming and lower rainfall']},
 {topic:5,prompt:'A diagram shows arrows between a pond, a cloud and the land. A student uses it to predict exactly how much water the pond will contain next month. Explain what is missing from the model.',help:'Give a missing detail and explain why it matters.',answer:'The arrows show routes but do not give the amounts or rates of water entering and leaving the pond. The model would also need the starting volume and information about changing rainfall and evaporation. Without these quantities and a timescale, it cannot predict the pond’s volume next month.',criteria:['A specific missing quantity identified','Its effect on the prediction explained','A distinction between showing pathways and predicting amounts']}
];

const STORAGE_KEY='stp-y9-spheres-revision-v1';
function readProgress(){try{const p=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY)||'{}');return {confidence:p.confidence||{},best:p.best||{}};}catch{return {confidence:{},best:{}};}}
let progress=readProgress(), topicIndex=0, mode='learn', quiz=null, processIndex=0, cloudStage=0, wind='west', storeIndex=0;
const $=s=>document.querySelector(s);
function save(){try{localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY,JSON.stringify(progress));}catch{/* Activities remain usable without storage. */}}
function shuffled(a){const out=[...a];for(let i=out.length-1;i>0;i--){const j=Math.floor(Math.random()*(i+1));[out[i],out[j]]=[out[j],out[i]];}return out;}
function escapeHtml(s){return String(s).replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));}
function setMode(next,scroll=true){mode=next;document.querySelectorAll('.mode-nav [data-mode]').forEach(b=>b.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(b.dataset.mode===mode)));render();if(scroll)$('#main').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'start'});}
function render(){if(mode==='learn')renderLearn();else if(mode==='quiz')renderQuiz();else if(mode==='explain')renderExplain();else renderPaper();}
function topicNav(){let n=Object.values(progress.confidence).filter(v=>v==='confident').length;return `<aside class="topic-nav" aria-label="Revision topics"><p class="nav-label">YOUR TOPICS</p>${TOPICS.map((t,i)=>`<button data-topic="${i}" aria-current="${i===topicIndex}"><span class="topic-num ${progress.confidence[i]==='confident'?'done':''}">${progress.confidence[i]==='confident'?'✓':String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span>${t.name}</button>`).join('')}<div class="progress-summary">${n} of 6 topics marked confident<div class="progress-track" aria-hidden="true"><span style="width:${n/6*100}%"></span></div></div></aside>`;}
function renderLearn(){const t=TOPICS[topicIndex];$('#workspace').innerHTML=`<div class="study-layout">${topicNav()}<article><p class="topic-topline">${t.lp} · TOPIC ${topicIndex+1} OF 6</p><h2>${t.name}</h2><p class="section-intro">${t.intro}</p><div id="diagram" class="figure-panel"></div><div class="notes">${t.notes.map(n=>`<section><h3>${n[0]}</h3><p>${n[1]}</p></section>`).join('')}</div><p class="note-correction"><b>Check your thinking.</b> ${t.fix}</p><section class="recall"><h3>Close your notes. Try these.</h3><p class="empty-note">Say or write your answer before opening the check.</p>${t.recall.map(r=>`<details><summary>${r[0]}</summary><p>${r[1]}</p></details>`).join('')}</section><div class="topic-bottom"><label class="confidence">My confidence<select id="confidence" aria-label="Confidence in ${t.name}"><option value="">Choose…</option><option value="revisit">I need to revisit this</option><option value="building">I am getting there</option><option value="confident">I can explain this</option></select></label><button class="primary" id="topic-quiz">Quiz this topic →</button>${topicIndex<5?'<button class="secondary" id="next-topic">Next topic →</button>':''}</div></article></div>`;$('#confidence').value=progress.confidence[topicIndex]||'';$('#confidence').onchange=e=>{progress.confidence[topicIndex]=e.target.value;save();renderLearn();};document.querySelectorAll('[data-topic]').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{topicIndex=Number(b.dataset.topic);renderLearn();});$('#topic-quiz').onclick=()=>startQuiz(String(topicIndex));if($('#next-topic'))$('#next-topic').onclick=()=>{topicIndex++;renderLearn();$('#main').scrollIntoView({block:'start'});};renderDiagram();}

